A||||9||JohnnyK||Need help with a Saturn.||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||03-04-2001||12:04 PM||||My friends Check Engine light came on in his new (to him) 1998 Saturn SL2 (SR2, I don't know whats it's called), and I was wondering if anyone knew where the diagnosis plug was, and if anyone knew what the codes were? I can't seem to find it anywhere. Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||03-04-2001||01:34 PM||||On the Saturns it's under the hood. On my 94 it was on the driverside in back of the battery. There was a panel with all the high current relays/fuses and it was next to them. I don't know if they moved them when they changed the body style. The codes are typical GM codes. You can get an index card free from AutoZone with the codes.||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||03-04-2001||01:35 PM||||So it will be the exact same as gm codes?||||reg||1
Z||000003||JohnnyK||03-04-2001||01:35 PM||||Sorry, Fiero codes?||||reg||1
Z||000004||pHoOl||03-04-2001||01:53 PM||||On my 92 saturn it was on the inside under the driver's side dash.  Unless i'm thinking of something different, i don't think that they ever put it under the hood.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who can't outrun the cops.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||03-04-2001||02:34 PM||||I could very well be wrong but don't you need  a code reader to look at the newer GM computer codes? ||||reg||1
Z||000006||JohnnyK||03-04-2001||03:20 PM||||I was going to use a paperclip. Is that not going to work? (Assuming the plug is the same as on Fieros)||||reg||1
Z||000007||JJ-85-2M6||03-04-2001||04:31 PM||||I have a Saturn, and in my experience the dealership will probably check the codes and give an estimate of repair cost for nothing. Of course the codes only give so much information as to what is wrong.  My 93 SC2 was showing egr solenoid and egr valve codes but the culprit was actually a cracked coolant temperature sensor. Once the collant temp sensor was replaced, no more codes...so the codes are only somewhat useful.  Just my 2 cents. ||||reg||10
Z||000008||Oreif||03-04-2001||05:32 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pHoOl:<BR><B>On my 92 saturn it was on the inside under the driver's side dash.  Unless i'm thinking of something different, i don't think that they ever put it under the hood.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>94 SL1 has it under the hood. It was put there so the dealer could hook up to the ECM while looking or adjusting the engine. That is how I checked the codes when I owned the car. I found it was there when the dealership mechanic hooked to it with the machine.<P>I don't think the Fiero codes will work. Fiero's are OBD I and everything from 1994 and up are OBD II. (If I remember correctly)<P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 03-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||JohnnyK||03-04-2001||05:32 PM||||Under the dash, however, it didn'tlook familiar so I didn't want to jump anything.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
